#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161856 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162056 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "405984cd-1e1b-54c6-bc75-268e127b9812" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Variance of a Constant"}] :question/id #uuid "01fc4e2e-d93f-50b3-859e-19732d4f10c2" :question/math-academy-id "e-9111" :question/problem "Calculate $\\textrm{Var}[4k^2-1]$ where $k$ is a constant." :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any constant $a,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[a] = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $k$ is a constant, $4k^2-1$ is also a constant, and we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[4k^2-1] = 0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161857 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162057 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a3752a0-431d-5520-97b1-493c36bbce54" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Variance of a Scaled Random Variable"}] :question/id #uuid "a3f88c0e-9426-50d2-baf0-95909e222d4b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9112" :question/problem "The probability distribution of the random variable $X$ is given below. Given that $\\textrm E[X] = 2.3,$ calculate $\\textrm{Var}[-10X].$ \n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.5$ |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any random variable $X$ and constant $a,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[aX] = a^2\\cdot\\textrm{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=-10.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[-10X] & =(-10)^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =100\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the variance of $X$ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm E[X^2] - \\textrm E[X]^2\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we're given that $\\textrm E [X] =2.3.$ \n\nWe start by computing $\\textrm E[X^2].$ Summing up the products of each value of $X^2$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={1}^{2}\\cdot f(1)+{2}^{2}\\cdot f(2)+{3}^{2}\\cdot f(3) \\\\  & =1(0.2)+4(0.3)+9(0.5) \\\\  & =5.9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =5.9-{(2.3)}^{2} \\\\  & =0.61.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[-10X] & =100\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =100\\cdot 0.61 \\\\  & =61.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161858 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162058 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85388f7d-b2ee-520b-b711-a7721c6f620c" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Variance of a Transformed Random Variable"}] :question/id #uuid "c20c7b15-e967-52a9-846d-4915f6661158" :question/math-academy-id "e-9114" :question/problem "If $\\textrm{Var}[X] = 10,$ calculate $\\textrm{Var}[1-2X].$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any random variable $X$ and constants $a$ and $b,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[aX+b] = a^2\\cdot\\textrm{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=-2$ and $b=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[1-2X] & =(-2)^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =4\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162263 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162056 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "405984cd-1e1b-54c6-bc75-268e127b9812" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Variance of a Constant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33994916-4232-5681-a751-4c386d44f6c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{k}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369025} {:answer/id #uuid "93325cb3-3cbe-5a46-acc2-fc1973572bf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369026} {:answer/id #uuid "4ece8c57-aa36-5399-a6b9-bdd774076d4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{k}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369027} {:answer/id #uuid "501ab2a2-7b1a-5df1-8bc2-0cf675ae859b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 175921860454